{
  "image_name": "a231ebf65f102405ca3aaba1ce867d73.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several experimental platforms for precision control and readout of molecules. Across the panels, molecules are first produced in a beam or source, then prepared in selected quantum states, manipulated with electric, magnetic, microwave, or optical fields, and finally detected through ions or fluorescence.\n\nOne approach uses a pulsed supersonic molecular beam entering a long segmented electrode structure. A rotating electric bias field guides the molecules through a wavy path, while shielding reduces magnetic and thermal disturbances; state readout is performed at the end with an ion detector. A second approach shows a cryogenic-like beam experiment in which a thermal YbF source, guide, laser cooling, and a helium beam create a “molecular fountain,” launching slow molecules upward between electric-field plates for longer interrogation.\n\nThe lower panels depict trapped or immobilized molecules. In an optical lattice or tweezer-like array, microwaves and laser light prepare and probe many trapped molecules, with fluorescence used for readout. Another scheme embeds molecules in an fcc matrix, where an applied electric field orients them. A small energy-level sketch indicates an orientation-dependent hyperfine shift, and fluorescence detection measures this shift while external electric and magnetic fields are applied. Overall, the figure highlights complementary ways to increase control time and sensitivity in molecular measurements.",
